findings.body:
`inputBoxHolds` is documented as "Whether the pane's input box still shows `message`". It actually returns `pasteLanded(...)`:

```ts
export function inputBoxHolds(pane: SessionPane, message: string): boolean {
  return pasteLanded(captureInputBox(pane), message);
}
```

`pasteLanded` (`src/claude/pane.utils.ts` L155-171) was built for `steerPane`, where `false` means "don't press Enter yet", so it returns false whenever it is unsure. It is true only when the box exists, does not match the `Try "` placeholder, starts with the message's first word and ends with its last. Its JSDoc says a mid-word wrap "is refused rather than guessed".

`tellConductor` (`src/core/conductor.service.ts` L275-279) negates it, so in the tell that same `false` means success:

```ts
submitPane(pane);
if (!inputBoxHolds(pane, message)) return true;
submitPane(pane);
return !inputBoxHolds(pane, message);
```

In each case below the tell skips the second Enter and the CLI prints `Told the conductor; the message landed.`:

- **A draft in front of the message.** The operator typed in the box via `pup attach` and left with Ctrl-b d. The tell never clears the box first, so `startsWith(first)` fails.
- **A first or last word wrapped across rows.** A long path or URL in a message of up to 800 characters, or a pane narrowed by an attach from a smaller terminal.
- **A paste that lost its head.** This is the decision-45 failure.
- **No input box on screen**, such as an open dialog. `inputBoxText` is `undefined`.

Decision 85 defines "landed" as "the box no longer holding it after the last Enter". That is not what this predicate tests.

A partial fix: test for any unsubmitted text after Enter (`hasUnsubmittedInput(captureInputBox(pane))`, already imported in this file). That covers the draft, wrap and missing-head cases. The no-box case still needs pane.utils to tell "no box" apart from "empty box", so the tell can report it as not landed.

